    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Five roles you already use — now with names",
      "body": [
        "Since A1 you've built sentences like الْأَبُ سَعِيدٌ (\"the father is happy\") without ever naming the two halves: الْأَبُ is the مُبْتَدَأ (mubtadaʾ, \"topic\") and سَعِيدٌ is the خَبَر (khabar, \"comment\") — the thing you're talking about, and what you say about it.",
        "A2 added verb-first sentences and iḍāfa phrases, each with their own named roles: in ذَهَبَ الرَّجُلُ (\"the man went\"), الرَّجُلُ is the فَاعِل (fāʿil, \"doer\"); in قَرَأْتُ الْكِتَابَ (\"I read the book\"), الْكِتَابَ is the مَفْعُول بِهِ (mafʿūl bih, \"object\"); in بَابُ الْبَيْتِ (\"the door of the house\"), الْبَيْتِ is the مُضَاف إِلَيْهِ (muḍāf ilayhi, \"possessor\").",
        "This lesson doesn't teach anything new about how Arabic works — it names five roles you've already been building correctly, on seven sentences you've already read. Full case marking (رفع/نصب/جر) on top of these roles is B2's job."
      ],
